473	# Workflow format
474	
475	This page describes the workflow JSON that defines a Freya agent. Read it before you write a builder. Tools, API calls and code are in [tools-and-code.md](tools-and-code.md). The REST calls are in [api.md](api.md).
476	
477	Some statements are marked "verified in a working builder". Those come from a builder that created a 6-node workflow through the REST API and whose agent passed its practice cases.
478	
479	## What a workflow is
480	
481	A workflow describes a conversation as a graph. Each **node** is one step of the conversation and has its own prompt. **Transitions** move the conversation from one node to the next. A transition fires on what the user said or on the value of a variable.
482	
483	When you create a workflow through the API, the platform creates an agent for it. You run simulations against that agent.
484	
485	## Three model calls per turn
486	
487	After each user message the platform makes three separate model calls. They do not share a prompt.
488	
489	```text
490	user message
491	    |
492	    v
493	1. EXTRACTION   reads the message, stores variables
494	2. ROUTING      decides whether to leave the node
495	3. DIALOGUE     writes the agent's reply
496	```
497	
498	| Call | What it sees | What it produces |
499	|---|---|---|
500	| Extraction | Recent conversation messages and the current node's `extraction_schema` | Variable values |
501	| Routing | Recent conversation messages and the transition descriptions. It does not see the dialogue prompt | The transition to take, or none |
502	| Dialogue | `system_prompt`, `role_message`, `task_message`, the full conversation history, and the node's `tools` | The reply, or a tool call |
503	
504	Consequences:
505	
506	- A routing instruction inside `task_message` has no effect on routing. Routing reads only transitions.
507	- The dialogue model can call only the tools in the current node's `tools` array. Everything else (API calls on entry, code, ending the call) is run by the workflow engine.
508	- Deterministic conditions (variable checks) need no model call. They are evaluated in code.
509	
510	## Top-level keys
511	
512	```json
513	{
514	  "initial_node": "greeting",
515	  "system_prompt": "You are the assistant of a dental clinic. Speak English.",
516	  "nodes": {},
517	  "global_nodes": {},
518	  "test_variables": { "customer_id": "c_test" }
519	}
520	```
521	
522	| Key | Type | Required | Meaning |
523	|---|---|---|---|
524	| `initial_node` | string | yes | Key of the node where every call starts |
525	| `nodes` | object | yes | Map of node key to node definition |
526	| `system_prompt` | string | no | Global rules. Part of the dialogue prompt in every node |
527	| `global_nodes` | object | no | Interrupt handlers that can fire from any node. See "Global nodes" |
528	| `test_variables` | object | no | Default values for call variables. See "Variables" |
529	| `context_variables` | object | no | A state block of chosen variables for the dialogue model. See "Context variables" |
530	| `tools` | array | no | Workflow-level lifecycle tools. See tools-and-code.md |
531	| `agent_config` | object | no | Model and call settings. See below |
532	| `nodePositions` | object | no | Layout for the visual editor. The working builder omits it |
533	
534	**`agent_config`**: omit it. The platform fills in defaults. It holds model, voice and timeout settings that a builder for this event does not need. In a simulation you choose the models with the override fields described in api.md.
535	
536	**No `null` values.** The schema is strict. Leave an optional field out, or set an optional string to `""`. A `null` fails validation.
537	
538	The REST API takes the workflow inside a request body `{"name": "...", "workflow": {...}}`. The object described on this page is the value of `workflow`.
539	
540	## Node types
541	
542	| `node_type` | Purpose | Speaks to the user | Carries |
543	|---|---|---|---|
544	| `conversation` | A dialogue step | yes | `task_message`, `tools`, `extraction_schema`, `transitions` |
545	| `api_request` | Runs an HTTP call on entry | usually not | `pre_actions`, node-level `response_mapping`, `transitions` |
546	| `code` | Runs a Python snippet on entry | usually not | `pre_actions` with a `code_exec` function, `transitions` |
547	| `end_call` | Ends the call | optional farewell | `terminal` |
548	
549	Other node types exist: `transfer_call`, `integration`, `knowledge_base`, `reschedule_call`, `invoke_agent`, `exit`. Telephony, voice settings, knowledge base and integrations also exist on the platform. You do not need any of them here and this page does not describe them.
550	
551	The platform strips fields that do not belong to a node type, without an error:
552	
553	- `tools` is kept only on `conversation` nodes.
554	- `pre_actions` is kept only on `api_request` and `code` nodes (and the other non-conversation action types).
555	
556	So a node cannot have both. If a call must run before a conversation node, put it in its own `api_request` node in front.
557	
558	## Node fields
559	
560	| Field | Type | Default | Meaning |
561	|---|---|---|---|
562	| `name` | string | | Node name. Use the same value as the node's key, in `snake_case` |
563	| `node_type` | string | | One of the types above |
564	| `task_message` | string | | The instruction for the dialogue model in this node |
565	| `role_message` | string | | Extra persona text for this node. Rarely needed |
566	| `on_enter` | object | `{"type": "llm"}` | What happens on entry. See "Entry behaviour" |
567	| `on_enter_first_entry_only` | boolean | `false` | Run the entry behaviour only on the first visit |
568	| `transitions` | array | | Outgoing transitions, in priority order |
569	| `routing_prompt` | string | | Extra context for the routing model in this node |
570	| `loop_condition` | string | | Intent description for staying in the node. See "Loop conditions" |
571	| `loop_prompt` | string | | Replaces `task_message` from the second turn in the node |
572	| `extraction_schema` | array | | Variables to extract from user speech in this node |
573	| `tools` | array | | Tools the dialogue model may call. `conversation` nodes only |
574	| `pre_actions` | array | | Calls the engine runs on entry. `api_request` and `code` nodes |
575	| `response_mapping` | array | | For `pre_actions` nodes: maps the call result to variables. Node level |
576	| `passthrough` | boolean | `false` | Route right after entry, without waiting for the user |
577	| `passthrough_default_transition` | string | | Target node for a passthrough node |
578	| `max_node_turns` | integer | `10` | Cap on user turns spent in the node |
579	| `max_turn_transition` | string | | Target node when the cap is reached |
580	| `terminal` | object | | The end-call function. `end_call` nodes only |
581	| `disable_inactivity_monitor` | boolean | `false` | Switch off silence check-ins for this node |
582	| `suppress_tool_results` | boolean | `false` | Hide raw call results from the dialogue model. See tools-and-code.md |
583	| `truncation_strategy` | string | `keep_all` | How much history the dialogue model sees. See "Limits" |
584	| `truncation_window_size` | integer | `10` | Number of recent messages kept by `keep_recent` and `summarize` |
585	| `reasoning_override` | string | `auto` | `enabled` or `disabled` forces model reasoning on or off for this node. Only models that support such a switch react to it |
586	
587	A per-node `idle_config` (silence timeouts) and per-node speech settings also exist. They matter on voice calls only.
588	
589	## Prompts
590	
591	The dialogue model receives one system message built from three parts:
592	
593	```text
594	system_prompt        workflow level, the same in every node
595	role_message         node level, optional
596	## Current Task
597	task_message         node level, what to do now
598	```
599	
600	| Field | Level | Use it for |
601	|---|---|---|
602	| `system_prompt` | workflow | Identity, language, tone, rules that hold in every node |
603	| `role_message` | node | A different persona for one node. Most workflows leave it out |
604	| `task_message` | node | What the agent must do in this step |
605	| `loop_prompt` | node | A replacement for `task_message` after the first turn in the node |
606	| `on_enter` | node | What the agent does when it enters the node |
607	
608	Rules for `task_message`:
609	
610	- Write instructions for the model, not a script.
611	- Cover this node's job only.
612	- Do not describe routing or name other nodes. The model does not know about nodes. Text such as "then move to the lookup node" makes it narrate steps that have not happened.
613	- If the node has tools, refer to them by name and say when to call them. Do not repeat the tool descriptions.
614	- In a node without tools, state that no tool call is allowed in this turn. If the node has a `loop_prompt`, put that rule in both prompts.
615	
616	Example:
617	
618	```json
619	{
620	  "name": "collect_details",
621	  "node_type": "conversation",
622	  "task_message": "Ask which service the caller wants and on which day. Ask one question at a time. If the caller is unsure about the day, offer to check the next free days.",
623	  "transitions": []
624	}
625	```
626	
627	### Entry behaviour
628	
629	| `on_enter` | Behaviour |
630	|---|---|
631	| `{"type": "llm"}` | The model writes the first line from `task_message`. Default. An optional `prompt` key overrides the entry instruction for this node |
632	| `{"type": "tts", "text": "..."}` | A fixed line is spoken. No model call. `{{var}}` works in `text` |
633	| `{"type": "silent"}` | Nothing is said. The node waits for the user, or routes at once if it is a passthrough node |
634	| `{"type": "audio", "asset_id": "..."}` | Plays a recorded asset. Not needed here |
635	
636	Set `on_enter_first_entry_only` to `true` when a node can be re-entered and must not repeat its opening line. This works better than a prompt rule such as "do not greet twice".
637	
638	### History across nodes
639	
640	When the conversation moves from node A to node B:
641	
642	- User and assistant messages are kept.
643	- The system message is replaced by node B's prompts.
644	- A loop prompt from node A is removed.
645	
646	So node B can refer to anything said earlier. Variables also stay for the whole call.
647	
648	## Variables
649	
650	All variables live in one table for the whole call. There is no per-node scope. A variable is never cleared on a transition. A later write with the same name overwrites it.
651	
652	| Source | How it gets in |
653	|---|---|
654	| Call variables | Passed when the call or simulation starts |
655	| `test_variables` | Workflow-level defaults for call variables |
656	| Extraction | `extraction_schema` on a node |
657	| Call results | `response_mapping`, and the automatic result keys described in tools-and-code.md |
658	| Code | A code node or `code_exec` tool writes into `vars` |
659	| System | Set by the platform |
660	
661	### `{{var}}` substitution
662	
663	Write `{{variable_name}}` in prompts, `on_enter.text`, transition descriptions, URLs, headers and request bodies. The engine replaces it before the model or the HTTP call sees the text.
664	
665	| Form | Result |
666	|---|---|
667	| `{{customer_name}}` | The value as a string |
668	| `{{amount:json}}` | The value JSON-encoded. Use it inside request bodies |
669	
670	Behaviour to know:
671	
672	- Date values are formatted for speech in the agent's language when they are substituted into a prompt.
673	- An unresolved variable in a prompt stays as the literal text `{{variable_name}}`. The model sees the placeholder. In a fixed spoken line (`on_enter.text`, a terminal `spoken_message`) the agent says it aloud.
674	- In a field of an engine-run call (a `pre_actions` call or a lifecycle tool) an unresolved placeholder behaves in two ways. If the value is exactly one placeholder (`"{{x}}"`), the whole field is dropped from the request. If the value mixes text and a placeholder (`"Status: {{x}}"`), the request goes out with the literal `{{x}}` in it.
675	- Do not reference a variable in a mixed value unless an earlier node always sets it.
676	
677	### Call variables and `test_variables`
678	
679	Call variables are the inputs of one call, for example a customer id or the current time. In a simulation you pass them per persona in `customerVariablesMap` (see api.md).
680	
681	`test_variables` is a top-level object of default values. On a web call to the workflow it is merged into the call variables. A value passed with the call wins over a test variable of the same name.
682	
683	```json
684	{
685	  "test_variables": {
686	    "customer_id": "c_test",
687	    "first_name": "Dana",
688	    "now": "2026-03-11T10:00"
689	  }
690	}
691	```
692	
693	Verified in a working builder: call variables are readable as `{{customer_id}}` in prompts and tool parameters, and as `vars.get("customer_id")` in code.
694	
695	### System variables
696	
697	| Variable | Example | Meaning |
698	|---|---|---|
699	| `{{system.iso_utc_now}}` | `2026-03-11T14:30:00Z` | UTC timestamp of call start |
700	| `{{system.todays_date_en_cst}}` | `11 March 2026 Wednesday 08:30` | English date, UTC-6 |
701	| `{{system.todays_date_en_est}}` | `11 March 2026 Wednesday 09:30` | English date, UTC-5 |
702	| `{{system.todays_date_tr}}` | | Turkish date with day name and time, UTC+3 |
703	| `{{system.todays_date_tr.iso_8601}}` | `2026-03-11` | ISO date, UTC+3 |
704	| `{{system.conversation}}` | `AGENT: hello\nUSER: hi` | Transcript so far |
705	| `{{system.conversation.last-N}}` | | Last N turns. N is `1`, `5`, `10` or `20` |
706	| `{{call.direction}}` | `web` | `inbound`, `outbound`, `web` or `simulation`. A chat simulation in the trial showed `web` |
707	| `{{call.duration}}` | `42` | Whole seconds since call start |
708	| `{{call.call_id}}` | | Platform call id |
709	| `{{<node>.current_turns}}` | `2` | User turns in the node since the last entry. Resets on every transition |
710	| `{{<node>.total_turns}}` | `5` | User turns in the node over the whole call |
711	
712	There is no `{{current_date}}` placeholder. These system dates are the real clock. If your task supplies its own "now" as a call variable, use that variable.
713	
714	`{{system.conversation.last-N}}` is useful in a transition description or a `routing_prompt` when the routing model needs more than the last message.
715	
716	### Context variables
717	
718	Warning from a test on 10 October: a workflow saved over the API came back without this key, and the save gave no error. Do not rely on it. Write the values the model needs into the prompt with `{{variable}}` instead.
719	
720	`context_variables` is a top-level key. It shows chosen variables to the dialogue model as a state block, so you do not have to write variable tables into prompt text.
721	
722	```json
723	{
724	  "context_variables": {
725	    "pre_history_description": "Static call context",
726	    "post_history_description": "Live state",
727	    "items": [
728	      { "name": "customer_tier", "placement": "pre_history", "description": "Customer tier" },
729	      { "name": "attempt_count", "placement": "post_history" }
730	    ]
731	  }
732	}
733	```
734	
735	| Field | Meaning |
736	|---|---|
737	| `items[].name` | Name of a variable in the variable table. Must match `^[a-zA-Z_]\w*$` and be unique across both placements |
738	| `items[].placement` | `pre_history` or `post_history` |
739	| `items[].description` | Optional label |
740	| `pre_history_description`, `post_history_description` | Heading text of each section |
741	
742	Rules:
743	
744	- An item points at a variable. It does not create one. If nothing sets the variable, the row is skipped without an error.
745	- `pre_history` is written once into the system prompt. Use it only for values that never change during the call.
746	- `post_history` is rendered again on every turn after the chat history and is not stored in the history. Use it for values that change.
747	- The block is rendered as `<CONTEXT_VARIABLES>`, one line `- name (description): value` per populated item.
748	- No other keys are allowed on an item. There is no `value`, `type` or `default`.
749	- It does not affect extraction or tool parameter substitution.
750	
751	## Transitions
752	
753	A transition is an object in a node's `transitions` array.
754	
755	| Field | Type | Meaning |
756	|---|---|---|
757	| `target_node` | string | Key of the next node. May be the node itself. `__previous__` is allowed in global nodes |
758	| `description` | string | What the transition is for. The routing model reads it |
759	| `condition` | object | When the transition fires |
760	| `is_tool_call` | boolean | The dialogue model triggers the transition by calling a tool. See "Tool-call transitions" |
761	
762	### Condition operators
763	
764	Deterministic operators check a variable in code. No model call.
765	
766	| Operator | Needs | True when |
767	|---|---|---|
768	| `equals` | `variable`, `operand` | `str(value) == str(operand)` |
769	| `not_equals` | `variable`, `operand` | The inverse of `equals` |
770	| `greater_than` | `variable`, `operand` | `float(value) > float(operand)` |
771	| `less_than` | `variable`, `operand` | `float(value) < float(operand)` |
772	| `contains` | `variable`, `operand` | `operand` is a substring of the value, ignoring case |
773	| `is_set` | `variable` | The variable has any value |
774	| `is_not_set` | `variable` | The variable has no value |
775	| `always` | nothing | Always |
776	
777	The semantic operator uses the routing model.
778	
779	| Operator | Needs | True when |
780	|---|---|---|
781	| `matches_intent` | `description` | The routing model judges that the user's last message matches the description |
782	
783	All `matches_intent` conditions of one evaluation are judged in a single model call.
784	
785	### Condition shapes
786	
787	An unconditional transition:
788	
789	```json
790	{ "target_node": "next_step", "description": "always", "condition": { "operator": "always" } }
791	```
792	
793	An intent condition:
794	
795	```json
796	{
797	  "target_node": "complaint",
798	  "description": "The user wants to complain",
799	  "condition": { "operator": "matches_intent", "description": "The user is unhappy or wants to file a complaint" }
800	}
801	```
802	
803	Variable conditions go inside a group. The top-level group always uses `"logic": "or"`:
804	
805	```json
806	{
807	  "target_node": "order_found",
808	  "description": "The lookup returned an order",
809	  "condition": {
810	    "logic": "or",
811	    "conditions": [
812	      { "variable": "order_status", "operator": "equals", "operand": "shipped" },
813	      { "variable": "order_status", "operator": "equals", "operand": "delivered" }
814	    ]
815	  }
816	}
817	```
818	
819	For AND, nest a group inside the top-level OR. Never put `"logic": "and"` at the top level.
820	
821	```json
822	{
823	  "target_node": "priority_desk",
824	  "description": "Gold customer with a late order",
825	  "condition": {
826	    "logic": "or",
827	    "conditions": [
828	      {
829	        "logic": "and",
830	        "conditions": [
831	          { "variable": "customer_tier", "operator": "equals", "operand": "gold" },
832	          { "variable": "days_late", "operator": "greater_than", "operand": 3 }
833	        ]
834	      }
835	    ]
836	  }
837	}
838	```
839	
840	The working builder uses the bare form for `always` and the grouped form for every variable check. The internal reference also shows a bare single variable condition (`{"variable": ..., "operator": ..., "operand": ...}`) directly under `condition`.
841	
842	### Do not route on a boolean
843	
844	`equals` compares strings. A boolean can arrive as `"True"` or as `"true"`, depending on how it was written into the table. A mismatch fails silently: the branch never fires and nothing reports an error.
845	
846	- Prefer a string value such as `verified` or `rejected`, written by your code or by the API.
847	- If you must test a boolean that comes from an API response, test both spellings. Verified in a working builder:
848	
849	```json
850	{
851	  "target_node": "wrap_up",
852	  "description": "The booking call succeeded",
853	  "condition": {
854	    "logic": "or",
855	    "conditions": [
856	      { "variable": "booking_ok", "operator": "equals", "operand": "true" },
857	      { "variable": "booking_ok", "operator": "equals", "operand": "True" }
858	    ]
859	  }
860	}
861	```
862	
863	`greater_than` and `less_than` convert both sides to numbers, so `2` and `"2"` behave the same. Use them for counters.
864	
865	### Order of evaluation
866	
867	After each user message, routing runs in this order. The first match wins and routing stops.
868	
869	```text
870	1. loop_condition of the current node    match: stay in the node, use loop_prompt
871	2. the node's transitions, in list order match: go to target_node
872	3. global nodes                          match: enter the global node
873	4. nothing matched                       stay in the node, wait for the next user message
874	```
875	
876	Within step 2:
877	
878	- Transitions are checked in list order. Put specific conditions first and broad ones last.
879	- Deterministic conditions are evaluated first. If one matches, no routing model call is made.
880	- If several transitions of a node have the same `target_node`, the engine merges them at load time into one transition. Its condition is the OR of the originals. If one of them is unconditional, the merged transition is unconditional.
881	
882	Routing also runs after a tool result arrives. Verified in a working builder: a transition on a variable that a tool's `response_mapping` sets fires in the same turn, without a further user message.
883	
884	When nothing matches, there is no fallback. The agent stays and answers from the same `task_message`. Make sure every realistic path has a matching transition.
885	
886	### `routing_prompt`
887	
888	A node-level string. It replaces the default intent-matching instruction of the routing model for this node. Use it when the transition descriptions cannot carry the rule, for example "stay here until both the name and the date are given".
889	
890	### Self-transition
891	
892	A last transition with `always` that targets the node itself keeps the user in the node until an earlier transition matches.
893	
894	```json
895	{ "target_node": "collect_details", "description": "Keep collecting", "condition": { "operator": "always" } }
896	```
897	
898	Prefer this to `loop_condition` in most cases. All routing then lives in one ordered list.
899	
900	### Loop conditions
901	
902	`loop_condition` is an intent description. The routing model checks it before all transitions. When it matches, the agent stays in the node.
903	
904	`loop_prompt` replaces `task_message` in the system message from the second turn in the node. The model sees one of the two, never both. Write it so that it stands alone. `{{var}}` works in it, so it can show what was already collected.
905	
906	```json
907	{
908	  "name": "collect_contact",
909	  "node_type": "conversation",
910	  "task_message": "Ask for the caller's full name and email address.",
911	  "loop_condition": "The user has not yet given both a full name and an email address",
912	  "loop_prompt": "Name so far: {{customer_name}}. Email so far: {{email}}. Ask only for what is still missing. When both are present, read them back once.",
913	  "transitions": []
914	}
915	```
916	
917	Make the loop condition specific. "The user has not finished" matches everything and the agent never leaves.
918	
919	### Tool-call transitions
920	
921	Set `is_tool_call` to `true` on a transition. The dialogue model then sees the transition as a tool named `transition_to_<node>` and triggers it by calling that tool. The router does not evaluate it after each turn.
922	
923	```json
924	{
925	  "target_node": "confirm_booking",
926	  "description": "The caller accepted the offered slot",
927	  "is_tool_call": true,
928	  "condition": { "operator": "matches_intent", "description": "The caller accepted the offered slot" }
929	}
930	```
931	
932	Use it when the agent should decide to move, for example because of something it just said or computed. The agent can acknowledge and move in the same message. The target node's `on_enter` then asks the next question. Global nodes accept the same flag.
933	
934	### Passthrough nodes
935	
936	A node with `"passthrough": true` routes right after its entry actions. It does not wait for the user. `api_request` and `code` nodes are normally passthrough nodes.
937	
938	- Set `passthrough_default_transition` to the next node.
939	- End `transitions` with an `always` transition. A passthrough node with no matching transition deadlocks.
940	- Set `on_enter` to `{"type": "silent"}`, or to a short fixed line that covers the wait.
941	
942	```json
943	{
944	  "name": "prepare",
945	  "node_type": "code",
946	  "passthrough": true,
947	  "passthrough_default_transition": "greeting",
948	  "on_enter": { "type": "silent" },
949	  "pre_actions": [],
950	  "transitions": [
951	    { "target_node": "greeting", "description": "always", "condition": { "operator": "always" } }
952	  ]
953	}
954	```
955	
956	Verified in a working builder: a passthrough `code` node can be the `initial_node`. It runs before the agent says anything.
957	
958	### Global nodes
959	
960	A global node is an interrupt handler. It can fire from any node. It is checked only after the current node's own transitions found no match.
961	
962	A global node has the same fields as a normal node, plus:
963	
964	| Field | Meaning |
965	|---|---|
966	| `condition` | When the global node activates. Same format as a transition condition |
967	| `description` | Purpose of the node |
968	| `excluded_nodes` | Array of node keys where it must not fire |
969	| `is_tool_call` | Offer the global node to the dialogue model as a tool, not as a per-turn check |
970	
971	`__previous__` as a `target_node` or as `passthrough_default_transition` returns to the node that was interrupted.
972	
973	```json
974	{
975	  "global_nodes": {
976	    "off_topic": {
977	      "name": "off_topic",
978	      "description": "Brings the caller back to the subject",
979	      "condition": { "operator": "matches_intent", "description": "The user talks about something unrelated to their order" },
980	      "task_message": "Redirect the caller to the subject.",
981	      "on_enter": { "type": "tts", "text": "I can only help with your order. Let us continue." },
982	      "passthrough": true,
983	      "passthrough_default_transition": "__previous__",
984	      "excluded_nodes": ["goodbye"],
985	      "transitions": [
986	        { "target_node": "__previous__", "description": "always", "condition": { "operator": "always" } }
987	      ]
988	    }
989	  }
990	}
991	```
992	
993	Rules:
994	
995	- List terminal nodes in `excluded_nodes`, or the global node can loop.
996	- Exclude nodes where an interrupt would do harm, for example an identity check.
997	- For a plain redirect use a passthrough node with a fixed line. It is faster and more predictable than a model reply.
998	- Use global nodes for interrupts that can happen anywhere. Do not copy the same transition into every node.
999	
1000	## Extraction schema
1001	
1002	`extraction_schema` is an array on a node. After each user message the extraction model fills the listed variables from the conversation.
1003	
1004	```json
1005	{
1006	  "extraction_schema": [
1007	    {
1008	      "name": "order_number",
1009	      "type": "string",
1010	      "description": "The order number the caller reads out",
1011	      "validation_pattern": "^[0-9]{8}$",
1012	      "max_retries": 2,
1013	      "min_confidence": 0.8
1014	    },
1015	    {
1016	      "name": "request_type",
1017	      "type": "string",
1018	      "enum_values": ["status", "cancel", "change_address", "other"],
1019	      "description": "What the caller wants to do with the order",
1020	      "min_confidence": 0.7
1021	    }
1022	  ]
1023	}
1024	```
1025	
1026	| Field | Type | Meaning |
1027	|---|---|---|
1028	| `name` | string | Variable name |
1029	| `type` | string | `string`, `integer`, `float`, `boolean`, `date`, `datetime`, `name`, `phone_number` |
1030	| `description` | string | What the variable is |
1031	| `enum_values` | array | Allowed values. Works on every type. The old `enum` type is deprecated and becomes `string` |
1032	| `extraction_prompt` | string | A custom extraction template. See the warning below |
1033	| `validation_pattern` | string | Regular expression the value must match |
1034	| `min_confidence` | number | 0.0 to 1.0. Values below it are discarded. Default `0.95` |
1035	| `max_retries` | integer | Retries after a failed validation, with the error given to the model |
1036	
1037	How it works:
1038	
1039	1. The extraction model sees recent messages and the schema.
1040	2. It returns structured values.
1041	3. Confidence is computed from the model's token probabilities, not from a self-report.
1042	4. Each value is checked against `validation_pattern` and `enum_values`.
1043	5. On failure the model retries, up to `max_retries`.
1044	
1045	Rules:
1046	
1047	- **Set `min_confidence` on every variable.** The default of 0.95 is strict. A discarded value leaves no trace in the transcript, so it looks the same as "nothing was extracted".
1048	- **`extraction_prompt` replaces the whole built-in template.** It is not appended. A custom prompt must contain the input placeholders itself: `{{extractor.speech}}`, `{{extractor.conversation.last-10}}`, `{{extractor.type_preamble}}` and `{{extractor.variables_list}}`. Without them the extractor never sees the user's message and returns nothing on every turn, while dialogue and routing look healthy.
1049	- **Declare dates as `type: "date"`.** The date types give the extractor today's date and the rules for relative dates such as "tomorrow". A date typed as `string` gets no date anchor and the model guesses the year.
1050	- The built-in date rules turn vague expressions such as "next week" into a concrete date. If you need vague dates rejected, a custom prompt must say so and state that this overrides the general rules.
1051	- No `null`. Omit optional fields or use `""`.
1052	- An extracted variable is available to the same node's transitions in the same turn, and to every later node.
1053	
1054	Inside `extraction_prompt` these date placeholders resolve: `{{date.today}}`, `{{time.now}}`, `{{date.day_of_week}}`, `{{date.yesterday}}`, `{{date.tomorrow}}`, `{{date.day_after_tomorrow}}`, `{{date.next_week}}`, `{{date.end_of_month}}`, `{{date.first_next_month}}`, `{{date.next_mon}}` to `{{date.next_sun}}`, `{{date.last_mon}}` to `{{date.last_sun}}`, `{{date.fnm_mon}}` to `{{date.fnm_sun}}` (first such weekday of next month), `{{date.weeks2}}`, `{{date.weeks3}}`, `{{date.weeks2_ago}}`, `{{date.weeks3_ago}}`. Do not rely on them outside extraction prompts.
1055	
1056	**Fork on an extracted enum.** To route from one node to several paths, extract an enum and branch with `equals`. This is more reliable than several competing `matches_intent` transitions.
1057	
1058	```json
1059	{
1060	  "name": "menu",
1061	  "node_type": "conversation",
1062	  "task_message": "Tell the caller the order status in one sentence. Then ask whether they want to change the delivery address, cancel the order, or end the call.",
1063	  "extraction_schema": [
1064	    {
1065	      "name": "next_action",
1066	      "type": "string",
1067	      "enum_values": ["change_address", "cancel", "done"],
1068	      "description": "What the caller chose. Empty if unclear.",
1069	      "min_confidence": 0.7
1070	    }
1071	  ],
1072	  "transitions": [
1073	    { "target_node": "change_address", "description": "Change address", "condition": { "logic": "or", "conditions": [ { "variable": "next_action", "operator": "equals", "operand": "change_address" } ] } },
1074	    { "target_node": "cancel_order", "description": "Cancel", "condition": { "logic": "or", "conditions": [ { "variable": "next_action", "operator": "equals", "operand": "cancel" } ] } },
1075	    { "target_node": "goodbye", "description": "Done", "condition": { "logic": "or", "conditions": [ { "variable": "next_action", "operator": "equals", "operand": "done" } ] } },
1076	    { "target_node": "menu", "description": "Unclear, stay", "condition": { "operator": "always" } }
1077	  ]
1078	}
1079	```
1080	
1081	Use extraction for user speech. For API responses use `response_mapping` (tools-and-code.md). It is deterministic and needs no model call.
1082	
1083	## End-call nodes
1084	
1085	An `end_call` node hangs up. It has a `terminal` object and no outgoing transitions.
1086	
1087	```json
1088	{
1089	  "name": "goodbye",
1090	  "node_type": "end_call",
1091	  "on_enter": { "type": "silent" },
1092	  "disable_inactivity_monitor": true,
1093	  "transitions": [],
1094	  "terminal": {
1095	    "type": "function",
1096	    "function": {
1097	      "name": "closing_end",
1098	      "handler": "__function__:end_call",
1099	      "parameters": {
1100	        "type": "object",
1101	        "properties": {
1102	          "message_mode": { "type": "string", "default": "custom" },
1103	          "spoken_message": { "type": "string", "default": "Thank you for calling. Goodbye." }
1104	        }
1105	      }
1106	    }
1107	  }
1108	}
1109	```
1110	
1111	| Parameter | Meaning |
1112	|---|---|
1113	| `message_mode` | `custom`: say `spoken_message`. `llm`: the model writes the farewell from `llm_prompt`. `none`: hang up without a farewell |
1114	| `spoken_message` | The fixed farewell for `custom`. `{{var}}` works |
1115	| `llm_prompt` | The instruction for `llm` |
1116	| `interruptible` | `true` opens a short window after the farewell in which the user can still speak. Default: hang up as soon as the agent stops speaking |
1117	| `trigger_timeout` | Length of that window in seconds |
1118	| `trigger_pattern_both` | Farewell phrases, separated by `\|`, that do not cancel the close. Anything else the user says in the window cancels the close and returns the turn to the agent |
1119	
1120	Rules:
1121	
1122	- **The farewell comes from the terminal, not from `on_enter`.** A node with an `on_enter` line and `message_mode: "none"` hangs up silently. Put the sentence in `spoken_message` and set `on_enter` to `{"type": "silent"}`.
1123	- Set `disable_inactivity_monitor` to `true` and `transitions` to `[]`.
1124	- **The call's `endedReason` is the terminal's `function.name`.** The node name does not affect it. Pick a name that describes the outcome.
1125	- Use several end-call nodes with different farewells and function names when outcomes differ.
1126	- If you set `interruptible`, also set `trigger_pattern_both`. Without a phrase list every polite reply cancels the hang-up. The window is timed on a wall clock and a chat simulation cannot measure it.
1127	
1128	Verified in a working builder: `message_mode: "none"` with a silent `on_enter` ends the call when the node is reached. The agent says its goodbye in the previous conversation node.
1129	
1130	## Limits
1131	
1132	| Limit | Default | Notes |
1133	|---|---|---|
1134	| `max_node_turns` | `10` | Cap on user turns in one node. Only user messages count. Tool results and agent messages do not. There is no "unlimited" value |
1135	| `max_turn_transition` | none | Where to go when the cap is reached. Without it the engine records the overrun and continues with normal routing, so the cap changes nothing |
1136	| `truncation_strategy` | `keep_all` | `keep_all`: full history. `keep_recent`: system message plus the last `truncation_window_size` messages. `summarize`: older messages are replaced by a model-written summary |
1137	| `truncation_window_size` | `10` | Used by `keep_recent` and `summarize` |
1138	| Extraction `min_confidence` | `0.95` | See "Extraction schema" |
1139	| Code `timeout_ms` | `30000` | See tools-and-code.md |
1140	
1141	Notes:
1142	
1143	- Truncation applies to the dialogue model only. Extraction and routing run before it.
1144	- Give every node that sets `max_node_turns` a `max_turn_transition`. The working builder sets `max_node_turns` to 60 and 80 on its conversation nodes without a `max_turn_transition`. Its calls ended well below those counts, so the behaviour at the cap is not confirmed by it.
1145	- Size the cap from real conversations. A node that handles a whole call can need dozens of user turns.
1146	- The maximum call length in a simulation is set with `maxCallDurationSeconds` (api.md).
1147	
1148	## Design rules
1149	
1150	- Use `snake_case` node keys that describe the step.
1151	- Keep dialogue in `task_message`, routing in `transitions`, data capture in `extraction_schema`.
1152	- Every path must reach an `end_call` node.
1153	- Put deterministic conditions before `matches_intent` conditions.
1154	- Every passthrough node ends with an `always` transition.
1155	- A transition back to an earlier node is supported.
1156	- Split a step into its own node when it has strict rules of its own or forms a clear state. Keep related optional actions together in one conversation node with several tools.
1157	
1158	## Complete example
1159	
1160	A clinic assistant. It collects a service and a day, looks up free slots, lets the model book one with a tool, and ends the call.
